          Yes, a properly sized HEPA air purifier removes much of the fine dust floating in a room, because a true HEPA filter captures at least 99.97 percent of particles at 0.3 microns and catches larger dust particles even more efficiently. It does nothing for dust that has already settled on floors, shelves and bedding, and the heaviest household dust settles within minutes, before a purifier ever pulls it in. So expect cleaner air and somewhat slower dust buildup, not a house that never needs dusting. Whether you notice the difference depends on matching the purifier's CADR to the room, putting it in the right place, running it constantly, and handling the dust sources a purifier cannot reach.
        </p>

        <div id="what-dust" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Kind of Dust Can an Air Purifier Actually Catch?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Household dust is a mix: skin flakes, clothing and carpet fibers, soil tracked in on shoes, pollen, pet dander, dust mite debris, cooking particles and soot. Those particles range from visible lint down to fine particles well under a micron. Size decides how long each piece stays in the air, and that decides what an air purifier can do about it, because a purifier only cleans air that it pulls through its filter.
          </p>

          <p>
            The big particles, roughly 10 microns and up, are the ones you see in a sunbeam and wipe off the coffee table. They are heavy enough to settle within minutes after being stirred up by walking, vacuuming or making the bed. Dust mite allergen is a classic example: it is carried on relatively large particles that become airborne when bedding is disturbed and settle again quickly. A purifier across the room catches only a fraction of these before they land.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fine particles are a different story. Particles of a few microns and smaller, including much of the pet dander, smoke, cooking particles and fine soil dust in a home, can stay suspended for hours. Those are the ones that reach deep into the lungs and that an air purifier handles well, because they stay in circulation long enough to be pulled through the filter many times over. This is the airborne dust a purifier genuinely reduces.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the honest answer has two halves. An air purifier reliably lowers the fine dust in the air you breathe, which is what matters for allergies, asthma and general air quality. It modestly reduces how fast visible dust builds up, because some particles get caught before they settle. It does nothing about dust already lying on surfaces, in carpets or in bedding. For that, the tools are a vacuum, a damp cloth and a washing machine.
          </p>

