        <div id="hepa" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Well Does a HEPA Filter Catch Dust?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Very well, if the filter is real HEPA and the air actually goes through it. The US Department of Energy standard defines a HEPA filter as one that removes at least 99.97 percent of particles 0.3 microns in diameter. That size is used because it is the hardest for this kind of filter to catch. Larger particles are caught by impaction and interception, and very small ones by diffusion, so a HEPA filter is more efficient above and below 0.3 microns, not less. Household dust falls squarely in the range HEPA handles best.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the labels. True HEPA or HEPA H13 means the filter meets a real standard. Phrases like HEPA-type, HEPA-style or 99 percent HEPA usually mean a filter that does not, and some of them perform far below true HEPA at fine particle sizes. For dust, a decent non-HEPA filter still catches much of the coarse material, but if you are buying a purifier specifically for fine dust and allergens, insist on true HEPA and a published CADR.
          </p>

          <p>
            The filter is only half the system. Air that leaks around a poorly sealed filter skips it entirely, and a fan that moves little air cleans little air no matter how good the filter is. That is why the clean air delivery rate, which measures the whole unit in a test chamber, matters more than the filter grade printed on the box. A unit with true HEPA and a weak fan can lose to one with a slightly lower-grade filter and much more airflow.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dust is hard on filters, so maintenance matters. Most purifiers have a washable or vacuumable prefilter that catches lint and pet hair before they clog the HEPA layer; clean it every few weeks in a dusty house. HEPA filters are typically replaced every 6 to 12 months, sooner with pets or renovation dust. A clogged filter still catches particles but moves less air, which quietly cuts the unit's real-world CADR. A gray, matted filter at replacement time is visible proof it was working.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Size Air Purifier Do You Need for Dust?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Size by CADR, the clean air delivery rate in cubic feet per minute, measured under the AHAM standard and printed on certified units. Every rated purifier lists three numbers: smoke, dust and pollen. For dust, the dust CADR is the most relevant figure, though the three tend to track each other. The common rule from AHAM is that the smoke CADR should be at least two-thirds of the room's floor area in square feet, assuming a ceiling around 8 feet.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice that means a 150 square foot bedroom needs a CADR of at least 100, a 300 square foot living room needs 200, and a 450 square foot open area needs 300. That sizing gives roughly five air changes per hour, which is a reasonable target for dust and allergies. Higher ceilings mean more air, so scale up. Our guide to <a href="/what-size-air-purifier-do-i-need/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">what size air purifier you need</a> walks through the math for unusual rooms.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Room Size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Minimum Smoke CADR</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Room</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">150 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100 CFM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small bedroom or home office</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">225 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">150 CFM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Primary bedroom</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">300 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200 CFM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Living room</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">450 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">300 CFM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Large family room or open plan section</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Undersizing is the most common reason people say an air purifier did nothing for their dust. A small desktop unit rated for 80 square feet, placed in a 400 square foot living room, simply cannot process enough air to make a measurable difference. It is also why one purifier rarely covers a whole open-plan floor. If you want results in two main rooms, two properly sized units beat one large unit parked in a hallway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy with headroom. The CADR on the label is measured at the highest fan speed, and most people run their purifier on a lower, quieter setting most of the time. Choosing a unit rated for a room one size larger than yours lets you run it on medium, at a noise level you can sleep next to, and still hit the target. That is usually a better buy than a smaller unit you have to run on its loudest setting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Where Should You Put an Air Purifier for Dust?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Put it in the room where you spend the most time, which for most people is the bedroom. Eight hours a night of cleaner air does more for allergy symptoms than a unit cleaning a living room you sit in for an hour. Close the bedroom door while it runs if you can; a purifier is cleaning a defined volume of air, and an open door to the rest of the house makes that volume much bigger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Give it room to breathe. Most manufacturers recommend keeping intakes and outlets clear by a foot or more, and away from corners, curtains and the space behind a couch. A purifier tucked in a corner recirculates the same pocket of air. Out in the open, with clear space on every side, it pulls in air from across the room and pushes cleaned air back into circulation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Place it toward the dust source when there is an obvious one. Near the entry where shoes come off, near a pet's favorite bed, or in the doorway of a room under renovation, it catches particles while they are still airborne. In a bedroom, a spot a few feet from the bed, not right next to the headboard, gives good circulation without blowing directly on you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid spots where it fights the room. Right next to an open window, it is cleaning outdoor air. Directly under a ceiling fan or in front of an HVAC return, the airflow patterns can work against it. And keep it off thick carpet with a bottom intake, where it pulls up carpet fibers and clogs the prefilter faster. A hard floor or a low stand solves that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="run-time" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Long Should You Run It, and Will You Have to Dust Less?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Run it all the time. Dust is produced continuously, by people moving, fabrics shedding, pets and air coming in from outside, so a purifier that runs only when you remember to switch it on is always starting from behind. Running continuously on auto or a medium setting keeps the fine particle level down steadily. A modern HEPA purifier on a medium setting typically uses a modest amount of electricity, less than an old incandescent bulb on many models, so the operating cost is mostly filters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Turn it up when you make dust. Vacuuming, dusting, changing bedding, sweeping and shaking out rugs all throw settled dust back into the air, where a purifier can actually catch it. Run the unit on high during cleaning and for half an hour to an hour afterward. That is the one moment when settled dust becomes airborne dust, and a purifier on high captures part of it instead of letting it all fall back onto the shelves.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set expectations on the visible dust. Most people with a correctly sized purifier report that surfaces collect dust more slowly, not that dust stops appearing. The coarse particles that make up most of the visible film settle before a purifier reaches them, and the air in the room is constantly being refilled from other rooms and from outdoors. If a lower dust rate on the furniture is your main goal, the cleaning habits and HVAC filter in the next section will do at least as much.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want proof it is working, a low-cost particulate monitor that reads PM2.5 is the best tool. Check the reading before you switch the purifier on and after it has run for an hour with the door closed; in most rooms the drop is obvious. A filter that turns gray over a few months tells the same story. What a monitor will not show is settled dust, which is another reminder of what a purifier does and does not do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-else" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Else Actually Reduces Dust in a House?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Upgrade the HVAC filter. A forced-air system moves the air in the whole house through one filter several times an hour while the fan runs, which makes it the largest air cleaner you own. A basic fiberglass filter protects the equipment and does little for dust. A pleated filter rated MERV 11 to 13 captures a much larger share of fine particles; EPA suggests MERV 13 or the highest rating your system can handle. Check that your system can take it, since some older or undersized systems lose airflow with dense filters, and change it on schedule.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vacuum with a HEPA-filtered, sealed machine at least weekly, more often with pets. A vacuum with a poor filter or a leaky body blows fine dust straight back into the room, which is why some people notice more dust after vacuuming. Damp-dust with microfiber instead of a dry feather duster, which mostly relaunches dust into the air. For workshop and renovation dust, our guide to <a href="/shop-vac-filters-explained/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">shop vac filters</a> explains which filters actually hold fine drywall and sanding dust.
          </p>

          <p>
            Control humidity. Dust mites, a major source of allergenic dust, thrive in humid air and struggle when indoor relative humidity stays below about 50 percent. Keeping a home between 30 and 50 percent, as EPA recommends, cuts mite populations and also keeps static and dryness in check. A cheap hygrometer tells you where you are; our <a href="/best-hygrometer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best hygrometer</a> picks are accurate enough to act on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then cut dust at the source. Shoes off at the door and a good entry mat stop a large share of tracked-in soil. Wash bedding weekly in hot water, around 130 degrees Fahrenheit, to kill mites, and use allergen-proof covers on mattresses and pillows. Fewer open shelves, fewer throw pillows and less clutter mean fewer surfaces to collect dust. Groom pets regularly, ideally outdoors. None of this is glamorous, but together it does more than any single gadget.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">The purifier handles the air. You still handle the surfaces.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A HEPA purifier sized to two-thirds of the room's square footage cleans the fine dust you breathe. A MERV 11 to 13 HVAC filter, a sealed HEPA vacuum, damp dusting and humidity below 50 percent handle the rest.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is an Air Purifier Worth It for Dust?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For people with dust allergies, asthma or pets, usually yes, as one part of a plan. It is the only tool that keeps working on the fine airborne particles between cleanings, and a bedroom unit running every night is a low-effort way to cut exposure where you spend a third of your time. If allergies are the reason you are shopping, our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-allergies/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best air purifiers for allergies</a> are sized with exactly this in mind.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is clearly worth it during dusty events: renovations, sanding, drywall work, or nearby construction. Fine construction dust stays airborne and drifts through a house, and a purifier in or just outside the work area, combined with plastic sheeting and a closed door, makes a real difference. The same applies during wildfire smoke episodes, which are a fine-particle problem a HEPA purifier is built for.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is not worth it as a substitute for cleaning, or as a fix for a whole house with a dirty HVAC filter and no doormat. If the main complaint is the dust film on furniture, spend first on a MERV 13 furnace filter and a good vacuum, then add a purifier where you sleep. And skip anything sold on ionizer or ozone claims. Ionizers mostly make particles stick to walls and furniture, and ozone is a lung irritant that EPA warns against; a plain HEPA filter is the proven technology.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, know the limits. A purifier does not remove gases like radon or most volatile organic compounds without a substantial carbon filter, and it does not fix moisture. If you have not tested your home for radon, the batch guide on <a href="/how-long-does-a-radon-test-take/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long a radon test takes</a> is a better use of a weekend than a second purifier. For a home that already has dust under control, our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-home/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best air purifiers for home</a> covers the units worth buying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a purifier that is too small</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A desktop unit rated for 80 square feet cannot clean the air in a 400 square foot living room. Match the smoke CADR to at least two-thirds of the room's square footage, buy a size up so it can run quietly on medium, and use one unit per main room rather than one for the whole floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Hiding it in a corner</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A purifier pushed into a corner or behind furniture recirculates the same small pocket of air and starves its own intake. Keep at least a foot or more of clear space around the intake and outlet, place it out in the room, and keep it off thick carpet if it draws air from the bottom.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it only when it looks dusty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dust is produced constantly, so a purifier switched on occasionally is always behind. Run it continuously on auto or medium, and turn it to high while vacuuming, dusting and changing bedding, when settled dust is thrown back into the air and can actually be caught.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring filter maintenance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A clogged prefilter and an overdue HEPA filter both cut airflow, which quietly lowers the unit's real cleaning rate. Clean the prefilter every few weeks in a dusty or pet household and replace the HEPA filter on the maker's schedule, typically every 6 to 12 months, sooner after renovations.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting it to replace cleaning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most visible dust is made of heavier particles that settle within minutes, long before a purifier pulls them in. A purifier slows how quickly surfaces collect dust, but it does not stop it. Pair it with a sealed HEPA vacuum, damp dusting and a better HVAC filter for a visible difference.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will an air purifier stop me from having to dust?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. It will usually slow how fast dust builds up, because it captures some particles before they settle, but most of the visible film on furniture is made of heavier particles that land within minutes. Expect to dust somewhat less often, not never. A better HVAC filter, a HEPA vacuum and an entry mat make a bigger difference to surfaces.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a HEPA air purifier good for dust mites?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Partly. Dust mites live in bedding, mattresses, carpets and upholstery, not in the air, and their allergen rides on relatively heavy particles that settle quickly. A HEPA purifier catches what becomes airborne when bedding is disturbed. Allergen-proof covers, weekly hot washes and humidity below about 50 percent do more to reduce mites themselves.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does an air purifier take to clean a room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A correctly sized purifier on high gives roughly four to five air changes per hour, so a noticeable drop in fine particles usually shows within 30 to 60 minutes with the door closed. That does not mean the room is dust-free; it means the airborne particle level has fallen and will stay lower as long as the unit keeps running.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an air purifier make dust worse?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not if it is a filtering purifier. Problems come from ionizers, which charge particles so they stick to walls and furniture, causing dark residue near the unit, and from ozone generators, which do not remove dust and irritate the lungs. A unit placed on thick carpet with a bottom intake can also stir up fibers. A plain HEPA purifier does not make dust worse.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I sleep with an air purifier running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, it is one of the best uses of one. You spend about a third of the day in the bedroom, so cleaner air there has the biggest effect on allergy and asthma symptoms. Choose a unit sized so it can hit its CADR on a low or medium setting, where most models are quiet enough to sleep next to.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do air purifiers help with drywall or sanding dust?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They help with the fine portion that stays airborne, which is the part that drifts through the house and settles everywhere later. They will not keep up with active sanding on their own. Seal off the work area with plastic, use a vacuum or dust extractor with a fine-dust filter at the tool, and run a purifier in or just outside the work area.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an air purifier better than a better furnace filter for dust?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They do different jobs. A MERV 11 to 13 furnace filter cleans air from the whole house whenever the system fan runs, which is efficient for overall dust. A portable purifier cleans one room continuously and to a higher standard. For most homes with forced air, upgrade the furnace filter first, then add a purifier in the bedroom.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

