        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Air purifiers do help with dust, specifically the fine dust floating in the air you breathe. A true HEPA unit sized to at least two-thirds of the room's square footage, placed in the open and run continuously, lowers airborne particles noticeably and slows the buildup on surfaces. It does not touch dust that has already settled, and the heavier particles that make up most of the visible film land before it can catch them.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For a home that feels less dusty, combine a bedroom purifier with a MERV 11 to 13 HVAC filter, a sealed HEPA vacuum, damp dusting and humidity between 30 and 50 percent. When you are ready to buy, our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-home/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best air purifiers for home</a> lists the units with the CADR to back up their claims.
